Define Embedding Layer
Below we define our Embeddings matrix using Flax. This is a very simple look-up table essentially that gets initialized using the LeCun Normal initialization method which is scaled standard normal distribution \(N(0, \frac{1}{d})\) where d is the dimension of our embeddings matrix.

class SkipGramEmbeddings(nn.Module):
    vocab_size: int
    embed_len: int

    @nn.compact
    def __call__(self, center: jnp.ndarray, context: jnp.ndarray) -&gt; tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:
        embedding = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.vocab_size, features=self.embed_len)
        word_embeds = embedding.embedding  # Retrieve embedding matrix
        return word_embeds[center], word_embeds[context]

Sequence log-probabilities
token_logprobs is reused from the PPO rollout-scoring code, where one forward pass over a finished sequence gives the log-probability of every next token at once. Summing its output under the response mask gives \(\log \pi(y \mid x)\) for the whole response.

def token_logprobs(model, sequences):
    """Log-probability the model assigns to each next token: output[:, t] scores sequences[:, t+1]."""
    logits = model(sequences[:, :-1])
    logps = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits, axis=-1)
    return jnp.take_along_axis(logps, sequences[:, 1:, None], axis=-1).squeeze(-1)


def sequence_logprob(model, sequences, response_mask):
    """log pi(y | x): the summed log-probability of the response tokens only."""
    return jnp.sum(token_logprobs(model, sequences) * response_mask, axis=-1)

Feed Forward Network
The feed-forward network is straightforward: two linear layers with a Gaussian Error Linear Unit (GELU) activation in between. The first layer expands the representation to d_ff (four times the model dimension) and the second projects it back down. Here we keep the network itself free of dropout, applying it instead at the block level, as we will see next.

class FeedForward(nnx.Module):
    """Position-wise feedforward network as used in Transformers"""
    def __init__(self, d_model, d_ff, *, rngs: nnx.Rngs):
        self.linear1 = nnx.Linear(d_model, d_ff, rngs=rngs)
        self.linear2 = nnx.Linear(d_ff, d_model, rngs=rngs)

    def __call__(self, x):
        x = self.linear1(x)
        x = jax.nn.gelu(x)
        return self.linear2(x)

Save Checkpoint
Once training is complete, we persist the model’s parameters with orbax so they can be reloaded later for inference or fine-tuning. Because the model was trained in a GPU-enabled cloud environment, its state is transferred to the CPU before serialization.

# Move to CPU before saving
save_directory = Path("./model_checkpoint/GPT_jax")

state = nnx.state(model)

def to_host(x):
    if isinstance(x, jax.Array):
        # Handle PRNG keys specially
        if jax.dtypes.issubdtype(x.dtype, jax.dtypes.prng_key):
            return np.array(jax.random.key_data(x))
        return np.array(x)
    return x

host_state = jax.tree_util.tree_map(to_host, state)

checkpointer = orbax.PyTreeCheckpointer()

checkpointer.save(
    save_directory.absolute(),
    args=orbax.args.PyTreeSave(host_state),
    force=True,
)
